        <p style="line-height: 1.6; margin: 0;">This circuit is a <strong>DIAC Relaxation Oscillator</strong> that generates periodic trigger pulses. The capacitor C1 charges exponentially through R1 from the 60V supply. When the capacitor voltage reaches the DIAC's breakover voltage (~30-35V), the DIAC switches from high impedance to low impedance state, causing rapid discharge of C1 through R2. This produces a sharp voltage pulse across R2 (V(out2)) and drops the capacitor voltage to a low value (~16V). The cycle then repeats as C1 begins charging again. The result is a <strong>sawtooth waveform</strong> at the capacitor and <strong>periodic trigger pulses</strong> at the output. This circuit is commonly used as a trigger generator for TRIACs in AC power control applications like dimmers and motor speed controllers.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="ch-card" style="background-color: #e8f8f5; border-left: 4px solid #1abc9c;">
